From: EglantinePS5
Subject: up: a measuring line for run-time pipeline build bursts, naming the thread whose requests grew the heap (EDEN_UP_BUILD_BURST_LOG), log only, off by default

What: on a true first start of a fresh install, about 2 s and again about 10 s after the house door
opens, the heap grows 450-810 MiB within 2 s (about 3,400 requests of 256 KiB-1 MiB), the pool falls
to 0-12 MiB and whatever asks next dies. A cold door builds 39-43 new pipelines in 10 s, a warm one
0-3 and has no wave. Nothing logs who owns that memory. With EDEN_UP_BUILD_BURST_LOG=1 a reporter
thread prints, every 2 s while run-time pipeline builds are queued, running or just finished, one
window line (builds started, in flight, pending, finished, failed, the most in flight at once; new,
on disk, evicted; heap pieces, large blocks and live use at start, peak and end; heap growth; the
count of non-arena requests under 1 MiB; the pool's largest free block and its low; main IR pools),
up to 12 thread lines (the heap records whose requests grew the heap most in that window, from the
existing per-record `grown` counter, with their thread names) and one job line per build (how much
heap its thread took since the build began: net, allocated and high-water). Only builds queued on a
VkPipelineBuilder (graphics, including the mesh compute passes, and compute) count as driver jobs;
the GPU thread's translation (kind 3) and translator threads (kind 2) are observations. Boot builds
are not watched. It changes nothing that is built, when, or how much memory anything gets.

Front part (headless/heap_arenas.inc, same commit): a 64-byte sidecar record per heap record
(512 x 64 = 32 KiB; eden_heap_thread unchanged) counts bytes allocated and freed by a thread only
while it is inside a build scope (eden_build_heap_begin/_read/_end), with a relaxed load and store
(single writer, the record's own thread); eden_build_heap_enable turns the counting on once.
Read-only exports eden_heap_record_growth (a record's `grown`, sidecar net and name) and
eden_heap_under1m_count (EDEN_HEAP_CLASSES under1M). The core reaches all of them as weak
functions; without the front the lines still come, with heap_hook=0 thread_hook=0 and thread=0.

Switch (read once, when the first pipeline cache is made): EDEN_UP_BUILD_BURST_LOG=1 (exact "1").
Log (the C stream stderr, so stderr.log on the console: snprintf into stack buffers and fputs, the
census's call; no heap use, nothing thrown):
"EDEN_BUILD_BURST event=on steady_ms= app_ms= window_ms=2000 reporter_stack_kib=256 heap_hook=
pool_hook= thread_hook= under1m_hook=" once; per window "EDEN_BUILD_BURST event=window window=N
start_ms= end_ms= app_start_ms= app_end_ms= partial= started= inflight= pending= finished= failed=
max_inflight= overflow= graphics= compute= mesh= new= disk= evicted= unknown= other_inflight=
growth_b= growth_mib= under1m_n= live_b= pool_b= pool_min_b= start_pieces_b= peak_pieces_b=
end_pieces_b= start_large_b= peak_large_b= end_large_b= start_live_b= peak_live_b= peak_main_b=
main_b= pieces_max_b= large_max_b= main_max_b= heap_hook= pool_hook= thread_hook=", then up to 12
"EDEN_BUILD_BURST event=thread window=N rec=R name=<name|?> grew_mib=G net_b=D" (most grown first,
then most net), then "EDEN_BUILD_BURST event=job window=N id= key= thread= kind= mesh= origin=
queued= done= success= begin_ms= end_ms= net_b= allocated_b= peak_b="; if the reporter thread cannot
be made "EDEN_BUILD_BURST event=off reason=no_reporter_thread error=E (switch stays off)"; if it
does not end within 1 s of the cache's teardown "EDEN_BUILD_BURST event=stop finished=0
waited_ms=1000". *_ms are steady-clock ms, app_*_ms the app clock (Common::g_wall_clock, the one
Eden::Performance::NowNs reads). A job's thread is its heap record + 1 (rec + 1 on a thread line).

Not in TranslationSwitches: no shader, SPIR-V or info changes.

Cost when off: Lifetime reads a function-local static once per cache (one getenv at the first);
per build 2-3 relaxed loads of a cached flag and disengaged std::optionals (graphics and compute
constructors, their builder jobs, the cache's two translation functions, TickFrame); no lock,
closure, thread_local, thread or allocation. Front: one relaxed load of a never-written flag per
heap charge. Fixed state about 48 KiB (core, never destroyed) and 32 KiB (front).
On: every heap charge on every thread loads the flag and its own record's depth; inside a build
scope also a load and store of net (and allocated) and a high-water compare, on the thread's own
cache line. One detached reporter thread with a 256 KiB stack, waking every 10 ms to check for its
stop and polling every 100 ms; at a window's open up to 512 lock-free record reads.

Default flip: none. This is a measuring line; it stays off unless a test asks for it.

Fixes against the first draft (two reviews): sidecar records _Alignas(64), early return when a
thread is not in a build scope, relaxed load + store instead of locked adds; Poll's row buffers
static (only the reporter calls Poll); the reporter is a detached pthread with an explicit 256 KiB
stack and its state is never destroyed (no joinable global std::thread for a static destructor),
the cache's teardown waits at most 1 s for its last window, and a thread that cannot be made turns
the switch off with one stderr line instead of throwing; under1m_n on the window line; up to 12
event=thread lines per window from `grown`; app_start_ms/app_end_ms on the app clock. The draft's
L1 (EDEN_UP_BUILD_BUDGET_MIB) and L2 (EDEN_UP_BUILD_RELEASE) are not placed.

Risks (on only): counting costs every allocating thread a little (measure the fps against the
switch off); `grown` names the thread whose request made the heap take a piece, not who holds the
memory; net counts only inside build scopes and only on the thread that frees; driver helper
threads show as their own records ("?" if unnamed); compute builds at run time are always "new"
(origin); the 2 s windows and the census are on different clocks (match on app_*_ms or steady_ms).

Print fix (T137, after T136's first cold start): T136 wrote every line with write(2) to descriptor 1.
On the console the app reopens stdout and stderr with freopen (heap.log, stderr.log) and the system
refuses dup2 and F_DUPFD (headless/log_flusher.h), so the streams do not keep descriptors 1 and 2 and
not one EDEN_BUILD_BURST line reached a log file. Every line now goes through std::fputs on the C
stream stderr (stderr.log, beside the EDEN_BIG_* and EDEN_MEM_CENSUS lines), the call the census's
reporter thread uses; the stream's buffer is already set (no allocation), Poll still writes outside
its mutex. Off path unchanged.

Origin and credit: our own. Drafted first as two drafts (core and front),
reviewed twice, every fix of both reviews placed.
